-- title:
--   The importance-sampling ratio corrects the returns: $\mathbb E_b[\rho_{t:T-1}G_t \mid S_t = s] = v_\pi(s)$ (5.4)
-- statement:
--   Let $b$ cover $\pi$, let $0 \le \gamma \le 1$, and assume that under $b$ every episode started at $s$ terminates within $H$ steps with probability one. Then the return of an episode generated by $b$, weighted by its importance-sampling ratio, has expectation equal to the value of $s$ under the target policy:
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb E_b\bigl[\rho_{0:T-1} G_0 \mid S_0 = s\bigr] = v_\pi(s) .
--   $$
--
--   This is why the first-visit ordinary importance-sampling estimator (5.5) is unbiased.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Time is shifted to $t = 0$. $v_\pi(s)$ is the expected return of an episode generated by $\pi$ from $s$ (not a Bellman fixed point). The book leaves termination implicit; the bounded-horizon hypothesis is a restriction relative to the book, which Example 5.5 (unbounded episodes) shows is not necessary in general.
-- source:
--   Sutton & Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction, 2nd ed., MIT Press (2018), ISBN 9780262039246, Eq. (5.4), p. 104

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SuttonBartoRL_ImportanceSampling_Episodes

namespace SuttonBartoRL.ImportanceSampling

/-- Sutton & Barto (2018), §5.5, p. 104, Eq. (5.4): `E_b[ρ_{t:T-1} G_t | S_t = s] = v_π(s)`.
Episodes start at `S_0 = s` and follow the behaviour policy `b`, which covers the target policy
`π`; under `b` every episode from `s` terminates within `H` steps with probability one; the
discount rate is `γ ∈ [0, 1]`. -/
theorem ordinary_importance_sampling_unbiased {S A : Type} [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Fintype A]
    (M : MDP S A) (term : Finset S) (π b : SuttonBartoRL.FiniteMDP.Policy S A) (hcov : Coverage π b)
    (γ : ℝ) (hγ0 : 0 ≤ γ) (hγ1 : γ ≤ 1) (s : S) (H : ℕ)
    (hH : ∑ n ∈ Finset.range (H + 1), ∑ e : Episode M n, episodeProb M term b s e = 1) :
    expectation M term b s (fun n e => e.isRatio π b n * e.ret γ) = value M term π γ s := by sorry

end SuttonBartoRL.ImportanceSampling
